Z||000000||bzzzted||06-18-2001||01:21 PM||||I am trying to gather information about brake conversions to get better braking on Fiero's, seems that main culprits of poor brakes seem to be 84-87, while 88 seems to have been given a better braking package. Maybe it is because I am somewhat mechanically defunct? but the whole GrandAm/ Beretta conversion seems off and somewhat complicated, what happens if i need new rotors? do i need to get them remachined after purchase to work again?<BR>On the other hand why can i not convert rotors from an '88 to work on my 84-87 car? i undersand that there are changes and differences in the years but is it possible to make this work?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Her86GT||06-18-2001||01:32 PM||||I have the Grand Am/Beretta break conversion on my car and it is absolutly amazing. I've had other Fiero owners test drive my car and they are sold on it.  Apparently, it's not that difficult.  Must husband (PBJ) is an auto mechanic for GM so he did the swap in about 2.5 hours and plans to do it to his.  The parts new cost about $250. cdn which includes pads, rotors, master cylinder etc.  It brakes better than our '99 GMC Jimmy.  If you have any questions on the conversion, post a topic on the technical threads and he will let you know all about it - or you can e-mail as well.  Where in Canada are you from?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar2sm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
